WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.460
Buy, set up and design like a professional maker.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:11.200
This is the video for Module 1 of Advanced Beadwork and Jewellery Making: professional materials, tools and design.

3
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.440
In the beginner course you strung beads onto cord and wire. Advanced work is different.

4
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:23.040
In bead weaving, every bead touches its neighbours, so uneven beads show immediately.

5
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:34.460
In wire work, the metal touches skin all day. The wrong metal causes green marks, tarnish or itchy ears. So buying well is the first professional skill.

6
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.870
You will learn to work out exactly how many beads to buy.

7
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.830
And you will plan pieces that feel comfortable on a real woman.

8
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:51.290
Findings are the metal parts: clasps, jump rings, ear wires and head pins. Read the stamp.

9
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:58.720
Sterling silver is 92.5% silver, mixed with another metal, usually copper, for strength.

10
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:11.130
14/20 G F means gold-filled: 14 karat gold bonded to brass, making up one twentieth, or 5%, of the weight. It lasts for years.

11
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.720
Gold-plated has a very thin layer that can rub off. Price it and describe it honestly as plated.

12
00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:26.810
Copper and brass are affordable and good for practice, but they darken and can leave green marks on some skin.

13
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:42.100
Nickel is one of the most common causes of skin allergy from jewellery. For earrings, use ear wires sold as nickel-free, or sterling silver, titanium or niobium, and tell the customer which metal you used.

14
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:51.840
Beads per gram change with brand and finish, so count a sample yourself on a digital scale that reads to 0.01 g.

15
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.090
Step one. Find how many beads your pattern needs.

16
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.870
Step two. Weigh 1 g of your beads, pour them on a bead mat and count them in groups of ten.

17
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.130
Step three. Divide the beads needed by your count to find the grams.

18
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.460
Step four. Add about 10% for spills, faulty beads and design changes.

19
00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.540
Step five. Round up to the seller's pack size.

20
00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:24.570
And buy it all from the same batch at the same time, because colours can vary slightly between batches.

21
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:34.720
Here is a worked example. Halima in Kaduna plans a peyote cuff. Her pattern needs about 3,000 cylinder beads in her main colour.

22
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.580
She weighs out 1 g of her beads and counts them: 186 beads.

23
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.390
3,000 divided by 186 gives 16.1 g.

24
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:54.170
She adds about 10%: 16.1 times 1.1 gives 17.7 g.

25
00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:09.270
The seller packs this colour in 5 g tubes. 17.7 divided by 5 is 3.5, so she buys 4 tubes, which is 20 g, all from one batch. She will not run short halfway through.

26
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.750
Put the flat side of your flush cutters toward the wire you keep, then feel the end and file it if it catches.

27
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:22.660
Cut ends can fly, so wear safety glasses, point the cutter down and cover the short end.

28
00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:36.180
Never cut steel memory wire with jewellery cutters; it dents the blades. Keep offcuts in a jar, away from children and bare feet. And use smooth-jawed pliers, because teeth mark wire.

29
00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:46.180
Give each piece one focal point and keep the rest quieter. Photograph it in black and white. If your eye goes to one spot first, it works.

30
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:53.870
Princess length, 43 to 48 cm, sits just below the collarbone and suits most necklines.

31
00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:04.870
For beaded bracelets, measure the wrist snugly and add about 1.5 to 2 cm. Record the customer's measurement, not just the size you made.

32
00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:15.270
Weigh each earring and wear the pair for two hours. If your lobe feels pulled, that is the common mistake: make it lighter or use a supported post.

33
00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:28.210
Use African bead traditions as inspiration with respect. Say honestly what your piece is, for example, inspired by Ndebele colour blocking, rather than claiming it is traditional.

34
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:47.400
To remember. Bead sizes run backwards, so 15/0 is tiny. Read the metal stamp and describe plated pieces honestly. Use nickel-free ear wires. Count a 1 g sample, add 10% and buy from one batch. And protect your eyes when you cut wire.

35
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:59.360
Now it is your turn. Build a materials reference card with your own 1 g bead count, set up and photograph your bench, and design and size one necklace on paper.

36
00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.650
Continue with the lessons below.
